 succeeded in 0ms:
    20	| | |
    21	|---|---|
    22	| OpenMS | the development commit pinned in `patches/openms.lock` (bd4b895, 2026-04-24; it identifies itself as a 3.6.0 pre-release), patched by `scripts/apply_openms_patches.sh` and built `WITH_OPENTIMS` to read Bruker `.d`. No released OpenMS package works |
    23	| Compiler | C++20 — GCC 13+ or Clang 16+ |
    24	| OpenMP | strongly recommended; without it the tool runs single-threaded |
    25	| CMake | 3.16+ |
    26	| **Memory** | **~57 GB peak** on a 30-minute gradient and **~170–190 GB** on a 2-hour acquisition at the shipped defaults (the 30–60 min cohort spanned 68–109 GB before the memory work); it scales with the acquisition |
    27	| Disk | mzML output is roughly 9 GB for a 30-minute run and 19–20 GB for a 2-hour run at the previous emission; the current default emits 30–70% more spectra. mzPeak is about a third of that |
    28	
    29	The memory figure is not a suggestion. A full diaPASEF acquisition holds millions of mass traces in
    30	flight; on a 32 GB machine the tool will not complete. Peak usage is reported at the end of every
    31	run, so you can size a node from your own data.
    32	
    33	## Install
    34	
    35	```bash
    36	git clone https://github.com/okohlbacher/spextractor.git
    37	cd spextractor
    38	scripts/apply_openms_patches.sh /path/to/OpenMS    # see "OpenMS patches" below
    39	cmake -B build -DOpenMS_DIR=/path/to/OpenMS/build
    40	cmake --build build -j
    41	```
    42	
    43	The binary is `build/spextractor`. `cmake --install build` puts it in `<install prefix>/bin` (set `-DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX`).
    44	
    45	### OpenMS patches
    46	
    47	`scripts/apply_openms_patches.sh` installs `src/TdfMzCalibration.h` into the OpenMS tree and applies
    48	three patches. **All three matter. The build stops without the first and the third; it does not
    49	notice a missing second:**
    50	
    51	- **Vendor m/z calibration.** Without it the Bruker reader converts flight time to m/z with a
    52	  two-point linear-in-sqrt chord that is **−5 to −11 ppm biased** (m/z dependent) on every file
    53	  measured, costing roughly **6–11% of closed-search peptide identifications**. Every emitted mzML
    54	  records which calibration was used in the `spx:mz_calibration` userParam
    55	  (`tdf_table_modeltype1` | `bruker_sdk` | `legacy_chord_APPROXIMATE`). An unsupported calibration
    56	  table **fails closed** rather than silently producing biased masses.
    57	- **Lock-free elution-peak detection.** OpenMS guards a shared vector with a program-global critical
    58	  section. Called from inside SpeXtractor's parallel window loop, that one lock serialises the tool.
    59	- **`MassTrace` move operations.** OpenMS declares a defaulted destructor and copy operations on
    60	  `MassTrace`, which suppresses the implicit moves, so every `std::move` deep-copied its points. A
    61	  `static_assert` stops the build without this one.
    62	
    63	Optionally, `OPENMS_BRUKER_SDK_PATH=/path/to/libtimsdata.so` uses Bruker's own library for the
    64	conversion instead. It is an independent cross-check, not a requirement, and is not redistributed.
    65	
    66	### Optional: `.mzpeak` input
    67	
    68	Build the mzPeak C++ library once against the same Arrow/Parquet/libzip that OpenMS uses
    69	(`scripts/build_mzpeak_lib.sh`), then configure with `-DMZPEAK_ROOT=<checkout>`. `.d` remains the
    70	primary input and is faster.
    71	
    72	## Run
    73	
    74	```bash
    75	spextractor -in sample.d -out pseudo.mzML -threads 64
    76	```
    77	
    78	That is the complete command. Every default is the configuration the project benchmarks; a run that
    79	needed extra flags to reproduce a published figure would be a bug, and there is a test for exactly
    80	that.
    81	
    82	Then search the output like any DDA file:
    83	
    73	          cmake --build OpenMS/build --target OpenMS
    74	
    75	      - name: Build SpeXtractor
    76	        run: |
    77	          cmake -S spextractor -B spextractor/build -G Ninja \
    78	            -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DOpenMS_DIR="$GITHUB_WORKSPACE/OpenMS/build" \
    79	            -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_LAUNCHER=ccache
    80	          cmake --build spextractor/build
    81	
    82	      # OpenMS refuses to start without its share/ directory (unit definitions, CV terms). A source
    83	      # build does not install it, so point at the tree directly. Without this the binary dies on
    84	      # --help with an uncaught exception whose message never reaches the log.
    85	      - name: The binary runs and reports itself
    86	        env:
    87	          OPENMS_DATA_PATH: ${{ github.workspace }}/OpenMS/share/OpenMS
    88	        run: |
    89	          spextractor/build/spextractor --help > help.txt 2>&1 || { cat help.txt; exit 1; }
    90	          head -5 help.txt
    91	          grep -q '^SpeXtractor' help.txt \
    92	            || { echo "the tool does not identify itself as SpeXtractor"; cat help.txt; exit 1; }
    93	
    94	      - name: End-to-end suite against the freshly built binary
    95	        env:
    96	          OPENMS_DATA_PATH: ${{ github.workspace }}/OpenMS/share/OpenMS
    97	        run: python3 spextractor/test/test_spextractor.py spextractor/build/spextractor

